3. Marketing Analytics — "did it work, and where should the money go"

This is the pillar Gartner rates. TruAudience offers unified SaaS-based tools for MMM, attribution and experimentation, with scenario planning and KPI-specific visualisations. 
TransUnion

Marketing Mix Modelling (MMM)
Plain terms: Top-down statistics. It looks at total spend per channel per week versus total sales, and separates out what marketing caused versus weather, price, competitors or the economy.
Example: "Of £100M in sales, TV drove £12M, search drove £8M, and £60M would have happened anyway." Works without any personal data, which is why it's back in fashion.
Technically it uses Bayesian inference, latent instrumental variables, recency weighting and time-varying coefficients — i.e. it accounts for the fact that a channel's effectiveness changes over time rather than assuming it's fixed. 
TransUnion

Multi-Touch Attribution (MTA)
Plain terms: Bottom-up, person-level. It follows an individual's actual path — saw a CTV ad, clicked a search ad, bought — and splits credit across those touches.
Example: "This customer's purchase gets 40% credit to CTV, 60% to search." Needs identity, which is where the graph earns its keep.

Incrementality testing / experimentation
Plain terms: A controlled experiment. Show ads to one group, hide them from a matched group, compare. The difference is the true lift.
Example: You find your branded search ads generate almost nothing incremental — those people would have bought anyway.

Scenario planning & optimisation
Plain terms: "If I move £5M from TV to CTV, what happens to sales?" A simulator built on the model.

Why all three matter: MMM is strategic but slow and broad; MTA is granular but breaks with cookie loss; experiments are truth but expensive. TransUnion's pitch is a balanced view across MMM, digital attribution and test-and-control experiments — use each to check the others. 
